# ==========================
# GLOBAL WORKSPACE RULES
# ==========================

You are generating code for a large, multi-tenant SaaS system called TAKA.
All instructions in this workspace must follow these rules.

# ==========================
# 1. CORE PRINCIPLES
# ==========================

- Always respect the architecture and requirements described in /docs/spec.md and /docs/PROJECT_OVERVIEW.md.
- Before coding any module, ALWAYS read the relevant spec section and confirm understanding.
- Work in small, atomic steps. DO NOT generate large patches unless explicitly asked.
- Always show a clear diff preview before applying changes.
- If something is unclear, ALWAYS ask clarifying questions.
- Never invent endpoints, fields, or models not documented in the spec unless asked to.
- Never refactor unrelated code unless explicitly instructed.
- Never run "global cleanup" or "fix all problems in the project" unless explicitly requested.

# ==========================
# 2. TECH STACK RULES
# ==========================

Backend stack:
- Next.js 15 (App Router)
- TypeScript
- Prisma ORM
- tRPC or Server Actions (if/when introduced)
- PostgreSQL (Supabase-hosted)

Frontend stack:
- React Server Components
- shadcn/ui
- Tailwind CSS
- Mobile-first UI required

Infrastructure:
- Deployment: Vercel
- Database: Supabase PostgreSQL
- All secrets come from environment variables.

# ==========================
# 3. VERCEL RUNTIME RULES
# ==========================

- All API routes must explicitly set Node.js runtime when needed:

  # Example:
  # export const runtime = "nodejs";

- Do NOT use Edge-incompatible Node APIs:
  # Forbidden:
  # fs, fs/promises
  # worker_threads
  # node-gyp bindings
  # process.nextTick
  # bcrypt native
  # sharp native

- Allowed replacements:
  # bcryptjs instead of bcrypt
  # wasm-compatible sharp if image processing is needed

- Backend logic must work in Vercel Serverless (Node.js runtime).

- For this Next.js App Router project:
  - Do NOT configure custom build outputDirectory in vercel.json for pages.
  - Rely on the standard Next.js build output (`.next` / hybrid output).
  - Do NOT change the Vercel build command unless explicitly instructed (it must stay `"next build"`).

# ==========================
# 4. DATABASE & PRISMA RULES
# ==========================

- Database provider is Supabase PostgreSQL.
- Must use DATABASE_URL from environment variables.
- Do NOT auto-migrate in Vercel builds.
- Use the standard PrismaClient guard:

  # Example:
  # import { PrismaClient } from "@prisma/client";
  # const globalForPrisma = globalThis as unknown as { prisma: PrismaClient };
  # export const prisma =
  #   globalForPrisma.prisma ||
  #   new PrismaClient({ log: ["query", "info", "warn", "error"] });
  # if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== "production") globalForPrisma.prisma = prisma;

- All tenant data must be scoped with tenantId.
- All Prisma models must match /docs/spec.md exactly.

- Prisma JSON rules:
  - Do NOT use `import type { Prisma }` if you need to access value-level members like `Prisma.JsonNull`. Use `import { Prisma } from "@prisma/client";`.
  - For JSON columns, prefer Prisma’s input types (e.g. `Prisma.InputJsonValue`) and `Prisma.JsonNull` for nullable JSON, instead of raw `null`.
  - When using `$transaction`, do NOT type the callback argument as `PrismaClient`. Let Prisma infer the correct transaction client type:
    # Correct:
    # await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => { ... });
    # NOT:
    # await prisma.$transaction(async (tx: PrismaClient) => { ... });

# ==========================
# 5. ROUTING RULES (Next.js App Router)
# ==========================

- Use the route groups:
  # (public)
  # (tenant)
  # (superadmin)

- Never create two routes that resolve to the same URL.
- Tenant routes require strict authorization checks.
- Public routes must NOT import server-only code.

- Next.js 15 specifics:
  - `headers()` and `cookies()` are async in App Router. Always `await` them in server components and route handlers.
  - Dynamic route params may be `string | string[]`. Always normalize:
    # const id = Array.isArray(params.id) ? params.id[0] : params.id;

# ==========================
# 6. SECURITY RULES
# ==========================

- Authentication uses secure cookies (or the auth mechanism defined in the current implementation).
- Server must always validate user/tenant context.
- Never trust client-sent headers without server verification.
- API responses must use consistent error structure.
- Never leak sensitive data (participants, payments, consents, B2B legal data) to client or logs.

# ==========================
# 7. UI RULES
# ==========================

- Use server components by default.
- Add "use client" only when required.
- Follow shadcn/ui conventions strictly.
- All pages must be fully responsive (mobile-first).

# ==========================
# 8. BUILD COMPATIBILITY RULES
# ==========================

- All code must compile on Vercel without modification.
- The default build script is:

  # "build": "next build"

  Do NOT change this script unless explicitly asked.

- When a Vercel build error appears, the agent must:
  - Identify the failing file(s),
  - Generate the smallest required fix in those specific files only,
  - Keep changes aligned with /docs/spec.md and the existing architecture,
  - NOT refactor unrelated code,
  - Request approval before applying large structural changes or touching multiple modules.

# ==========================
# 9. WORKFLOW RULES
# ==========================

- All changes must be incremental and explicitly approved.
- Code must be typed, readable, and consistent.
- No dead code or unused imports.
- The agent must ask for confirmation when:
  - Deleting any file,
  - Renaming any file,
  - Modifying authentication logic,
  - Changing multi-tenant architecture,
  - Adding new database models.

- The agent must NEVER:
  - Run global “fix all problems” operations,
  - Rewrite multiple unrelated files in one step,
  - Change version control settings or Git history.

# ==========================
# 10. ERROR PATTERN RULES (FROM HISTORY)
# ==========================

To avoid repeating past issues, the agent must:

- For Vercel:
  - Not introduce or rely on a `public` build output directory.
  - Assume this is a standard Next.js 15 App Router deployment.
- For Prisma JSON:
  - Avoid raw `null` in JSON fields when Prisma expects `NullableJsonNullValueInput`.
  - Prefer `Prisma.JsonNull` or omit the field when metadata is undefined.
- For typing:
  - Prefer inferred types in Prisma queries and `$transaction` callbacks instead of manual `PrismaClient` annotations.
  - Avoid converting type-only imports into value usage.
# ==========================
# 11. COMMAND EXECUTION RULES
# ==========================

# The agent MUST NOT run destructive or heavy commands inside the Cursor sandbox.

# Forbidden commands (NEVER RUN):
# - npm run build
# - npm run dev
# - npm install
# - npx prisma migrate dev
# - npx prisma migrate deploy
# - npx prisma db push
# - npx prisma generate
# - any command touching the database schema
# - any long-running or blocking shell commands

# Allowed commands:
# - none, unless explicitly permitted in writing

# Agent behavior:
- If the agent wants to run any of the forbidden commands, it must NOT request sandbox execution.
- Instead, the agent must output:  
  “⚠️ Build/migrate commands must be run manually on the developer's local machine. I will proceed without executing them.”

- The agent must NEVER request elevated permissions (full permissions run).
- The agent must NEVER re-run or re-queue build/migrate tasks automatically.
- The agent must NEVER attempt to resolve build errors inside Cursor by running commands.
- The agent must ALWAYS let the developer run builds locally.

# Workflow:
- Agent writes code.
- Developer runs:
  - npm install
  - prisma migrate dev
  - npm run dev
  - npm run build
- Agent reacts to resulting errors AFTER developer shares them.
